The container exists and is running, so it is not about web itself.

47
00:03:28,074 --> 00:03:33,009
(On some networks the host's DNS server answers for unknown names,

48
00:03:33,009 --> 00:03:38,693
and you see can't connect to remote host (127.0.53.53) or a timeout instead:

49
00:03:38,693 --> 00:03:48,340
the same problem, the name did not lead to the web container.) Look at which DNS server the client uses and whether it knows web:

50
00:03:50,100 --> 00:03:54,668
Put the containers on a user-defined network (lesson 048):

51
00:03:54,668 --> 00:03:58,054
Docker's DNS then resolves container names.

52
00:03:59,800 --> 00:04:00,513
Got it.

53
00:04:00,513 --> 00:04:03,264
What do I need to remember?

54
00:04:03,800 --> 00:04:07,639
Containers without --network join the default bridge network.

55
00:04:08,200 --> 00:04:14,719
On the default bridge, containers reach each other by IP only; names give bad address.

56
00:04:15,267 --> 00:04:19,030
Container IPs are not stable across recreation:

57
00:04:19,030 --> 00:04:20,711
never hard-code them.

58
00:04:22,467 --> 00:04:25,051
How does custom networks work?

59
00:04:25,600 --> 00:04:31,178
A user-defined network (docker network create NAME) is a bridge network you own.

60
00:04:31,178 --> 00:04:35,292
Compared with the default bridge it gives you three things:

61
00:04:35,292 --> 00:04:38,151
DNS (containers find each other by name),

62
00:04:38,151 --> 00:04:45,194
isolation (only the containers you attach can talk to each other) and control (you choose the subnet,

63
00:04:45,194 --> 00:04:48,401
and connect or disconnect running containers).

64
00:04:48,401 --> 00:04:52,654
Every multi-container application should get its own network.

65
00:04:54,400 --> 00:04:58,359
First, a fresh practice folder for this lesson.

66
