bad address 'db':

72
00:05:28,717 --> 00:05:34,893
Docker's DNS only answers names of containers on the same network as the one asking.

73
00:05:34,893 --> 00:05:37,981
Check which networks each container is on:

74
00:05:39,733 --> 00:05:43,482
When a container is on the wrong network (a common mistake),

75
00:05:43,482 --> 00:05:48,543
connect it to the right one; disconnect it from the networks it should not be on:

76
00:05:50,300 --> 00:05:51,013
Got it.

77
00:05:51,013 --> 00:05:53,764
What do I need to remember?

78
00:05:54,300 --> 00:06:01,219
docker network create makes a user-defined bridge with DNS, isolation and a subnet you can choose.

79
00:06:01,767 --> 00:06:06,131
Containers resolve each other's names only on networks they share.

80
00:06:06,667 --> 00:06:11,716
docker network connect/disconnect changes a running container's networks.

81
00:06:13,467 --> 00:06:16,911
Why would I need container-to-container communication?

82
00:06:17,467 --> 00:06:20,170
Real applications are several containers:

83
00:06:20,170 --> 00:06:23,137
an API and its cache, a worker and its queue.

84
00:06:23,137 --> 00:06:27,028
They talk to each other over a shared user-defined network,

85
00:06:27,028 --> 00:06:34,941
using the container name as the host name and the container port (the port the service listens on inside its container).

86
00:06:34,941 --> 00:06:38,831
Publishing with -p is only for traffic from outside Docker.

87
00:06:40,567 --> 00:06:44,526
First, a fresh practice folder for this lesson.

88
00:06:45,067 --> 00:06:50,996
One network, the cache, then the API configured to find the cache by name:

