105
00:08:05,947 --> 00:08:08,698
What do I need to remember?

106
00:08:09,233 --> 00:08:14,698
Containers on a shared user-defined network reach each other by name and container port.

107
00:08:15,233 --> 00:08:22,223
-p is only for traffic from outside Docker; internal services such as Redis need no published port.

108
00:08:22,767 --> 00:08:31,971
Pass dependency addresses as configuration (-e REDIS_HOST=redis); a 500 with a name-resolution error in the logs.

109
00:08:33,733 --> 00:08:36,913
What should I know about container DNS?

110
00:08:37,467 --> 00:08:44,365
On a user-defined network, every container uses Docker's embedded DNS server at 127.0.0.11.

111
00:08:44,365 --> 00:08:57,479
It answers the names of the containers on the same network (their container names and network aliases) and forwards every other name (example.com) to the host's DNS servers.

112
00:08:57,479 --> 00:09:05,211
Knowing exactly which names it knows explains most "could not resolve host" errors between containers.

