159
00:12:02,980 --> 00:12:05,731
What do I need to remember?

160
00:12:06,267 --> 00:12:12,576
EXPOSE is documentation of the listening port; it never makes anything reachable from the host.

161
00:12:13,133 --> 00:12:17,912
-p HOST:CONTAINER publishes a port; add 127.0.0.1:

162
00:12:17,912 --> 00:12:23,073
to keep it local; -P publishes exposed ports randomly.

163
00:12:23,633 --> 00:12:27,783
Containers on the same network reach each other's ports without -p.

164
00:12:29,533 --> 00:12:33,179
I keep hearing about network troubleshooting.

165
00:12:33,179 --> 00:12:34,718
What is it, really?

166
00:12:35,267 --> 00:12:39,176
"I can't reach my container" has a short list of causes,

167
00:12:39,176 --> 00:12:43,016
and each one has a command that proves or rules it out.

168
00:12:43,016 --> 00:12:44,761
Work from the outside in:

169
00:12:44,761 --> 00:12:48,113
is the container running, is the port published,

170
00:12:48,113 --> 00:12:51,394
is the application listening on the right port,

171
00:12:51,394 --> 00:12:58,305
is it listening on the right address, are client and server on the same network, is the name right?

172
00:12:58,305 --> 00:13:03,821
The classic trap is an application listening on 127.0.0.1 inside its container:

173
00:13:03,821 --> 00:13:07,451
it works from inside, and nothing else can reach it.

174
00:13:09,200 --> 00:13:13,159
First, a fresh practice folder for this lesson.

175
00:13:13,700 --> 00:13:17,289
From inside the container, the server works:

176
