179
00:13:27,027 --> 00:13:28,697
running and published.

180
00:13:28,697 --> 00:13:37,275
The host gets an empty reply (Docker accepted the connection on 8080 but nothing answered on the container side),

181
00:13:37,275 --> 00:13:39,780
and another container is refused.

182
00:13:39,780 --> 00:13:40,767
Step 3 and 4:

183
00:13:40,767 --> 00:13:43,728
what is listening inside the container?

184
00:13:45,467 --> 00:13:50,879
Make the application listen on all interfaces,

185
00:13:50,879 --> 00:13:58,643
0.0.0.0 (for this server --bind 0.0.0.0; for Flask --host 0.0.0.0,

186
00:13:58,643 --> 00:14:06,761
for Node server.listen(port, "0.0.0.0")), and recreate the container:

187
00:14:08,500 --> 00:14:09,213
Got it.

188
00:14:09,213 --> 00:14:11,964
What do I need to remember?

189
00:14:12,500 --> 00:14:14,594
Troubleshoot from the outside in:

190
00:14:14,594 --> 00:14:19,289
running → published → listening port → listening address → network → name.

191
00:14:19,833 --> 00:14:30,758
127.0.0.1 inside a container means only that container; servers in containers must listen on 0.0.0.0.

192
00:14:31,300 --> 00:14:36,779
--network container:NAME runs a debugging tool in another container's network namespace.
